**Glaucoma** is a group of eye conditions where the **optic nerve**, which connects the eye to the brain, becomes damaged. It is often linked to a build-up of pressure in the eye. Around **700,000 people** in the UK have glaucoma, and many do not know.

## Symptoms

The most common type, **open angle glaucoma**, develops slowly. It usually has **no symptoms** at first. It first affects the edges of your sight (peripheral vision), and the brain fills in the gaps, so you may not notice until a lot of sight is lost.

## Who is at risk?

- people over **40**
- people with a **close relative** with glaucoma
- people of **African, Caribbean or Asian** family origin
- people with diabetes or who are very short-sighted

## Getting diagnosed

Glaucoma is often found during a routine **eye test** at an optician. Tests include measuring eye pressure, checking the optic nerve and testing your field of vision. You will be referred to an eye clinic if glaucoma is suspected.

{% callout type="danger" title="Go to A&E straight away" %}
**Acute angle closure glaucoma** is an emergency. Go to A&E, or call 999, if you suddenly get:

- a very painful, red eye
- blurred sight, or halos around lights
- a headache with feeling or being sick
{% /callout %}

Read about [glaucoma treatment](/conditions/brain-nerves-and-senses/glaucoma/treatment/).
